---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import BreakEvenCalculator from '@/components/tools/finance/BreakEvenCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="break-even-calculator">
  <BreakEvenCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to use the break-even calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Add up your fixed costs for the period (a month, a quarter or a year) and enter the total: rent,
        salaried staff, software subscriptions, insurance, accounting.
      </li>
      <li>
        Enter the variable cost per unit: all costs that occur only because you made or sold one more
        unit. Materials, packaging, shipping, payment processing, per-unit labour, affiliate
        commission.
      </li>
      <li>
        Enter the selling price per unit without sales tax or VAT, because you collect tax for the tax
        authority and do not earn it.
      </li>
      <li>
        Optionally add a target profit and the units you expect to sell, to get the volume you need and
        your margin of safety.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>The formulas</h2>
    <p>
      Each unit you sell contributes its price minus its variable cost towards the fixed costs. This
      amount is the <strong>contribution margin</strong>. Break-even is the point where the
      contributions add up to the fixed costs:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>Contribution margin = price − variable cost per unit</code></li>
      <li><code>Contribution margin ratio = contribution margin / price</code></li>
      <li><code>Break-even units = fixed costs / contribution margin</code></li>
      <li><code>Break-even revenue = fixed costs / contribution margin ratio</code></li>
      <li><code>Units for a target profit = (fixed costs + target profit) / contribution margin</code></li>
      <li><code>Margin of safety = (expected units − break-even units) / expected units</code></li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How the margin changes the answer</h2>
    <p>With fixed costs of 50,000 for the period:</p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[520px] border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Price</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Variable cost</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Contribution</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">CM ratio</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Break-even units</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Break-even revenue</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="tabular-nums">
          <tr><td>10</td><td style="text-align:right">4</td><td style="text-align:right">6</td><td style="text-align:right">60%</td><td style="text-align:right">8,334</td><td style="text-align:right">83,333</td></tr>
          <tr><td>30</td><td style="text-align:right">12</td><td style="text-align:right">18</td><td style="text-align:right">60%</td><td style="text-align:right">2,778</td><td style="text-align:right">83,333</td></tr>
          <tr><td>30</td><td style="text-align:right">20</td><td style="text-align:right">10</td><td style="text-align:right">33.3%</td><td style="text-align:right">5,000</td><td style="text-align:right">150,000</td></tr>
          <tr><td>50</td><td style="text-align:right">20</td><td style="text-align:right">30</td><td style="text-align:right">60%</td><td style="text-align:right">1,667</td><td style="text-align:right">83,333</td></tr>
          <tr><td>100</td><td style="text-align:right">40</td><td style="text-align:right">60</td><td style="text-align:right">60%</td><td style="text-align:right">834</td><td style="text-align:right">83,333</td></tr>
          <tr><td>100</td><td style="text-align:right">70</td><td style="text-align:right">30</td><td style="text-align:right">30%</td><td style="text-align:right">1,667</td><td style="text-align:right">166,667</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      All rows with a 60% contribution margin ratio break even at the same revenue, 83,333, at any
      price. Break-even revenue depends only on the ratio; break-even units depend on the price. For
      this reason, defend the margin, not the price, when a customer asks for a discount.
    </p>

    <h2>Sorting fixed from variable</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[420px] border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Fixed</th>
            <th>Variable</th>
            <th>Semi-fixed (steps up)</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Rent and rates</td><td>Raw materials</td><td>Extra production shift</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Salaried staff</td><td>Packaging and shipping</td><td>Additional machine or vehicle</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Insurance</td><td>Payment processing fees</td><td>Second warehouse</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Software subscriptions</td><td>Sales commission</td><td>Support headcount per 1,000 customers</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Accounting and legal</td><td>Per-unit labour</td><td>Tiered hosting plan</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Semi-fixed costs can make one break-even number misleading. They are flat inside a range of
      volumes and jump at the boundary, so run this calculator once for each capacity band you might
      operate in.
    </p>

    <h2>Margin of safety: the number worth watching</h2>
    <p>
      Break-even shows where the floor is. Margin of safety shows how far above it you are. If you
      expect 3,500 units and break even at 2,223, your margin of safety is about 36%: sales can fall by
      more than a third before you lose money. A business at 5% is profitable but one bad quarter from
      trouble.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits of a single-product model</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Product mix: with several products, use a weighted average price and variable cost from your
        expected mix, or model each line separately and split the fixed costs. If the mix moves
        towards low-margin items, the real break-even point moves too.
      </li>
      <li>
        Volume discounts: materials usually cost less per unit at scale, so the real cost line bends
        down and the true break-even point is slightly lower than the straight-line model shows.
      </li>
      <li>
        Timing: break-even is an accounting concept. Cash break-even can come much later if customers
        pay on 60-day terms and suppliers want payment in 30.
      </li>
      <li>
        Inventory: the model assumes that you sell all you produce. Unsold stock holds cash and is
        eventually discounted, which lowers the effective price.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
